 "result": "**Mechanism:** a `contextvars.ContextVar` read through Werkzeug `LocalProxy` objects.\n\n- **`src/tessel/globals.py:40`** defines `_app_var: ContextVar[AppContext]`. It holds the current `AppContext`.\n- Four proxies are built on that one variable (`globals.py:41-62`):\n  - `active_app` is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"app\")`, so it resolves to `AppContext.app`, the `Tessel` instance.\n  - `g` is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"g\")`, so it resolves to `AppContext.g`, an `_AppCtxGlobals` object.\n  - `request` is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"request\")`, so it resolves to `AppContext.request`, a `Request`.\n  - `session` is built the same way, and `app_ctx` proxies the context object itself.\n- **`src/tessel/ctx.py:433`** is where the variable gets set. `AppContext.push()` calls `_app_var.set(self)` and stores the token in `self._cv_token`. `pop()` calls `_app_var.reset(self._cv_token)` (`ctx.py:498`).\n- Each proxy looks up `_app_var` and takes the named attribute on every access. So it returns whatever context is active in the current thread or async task.\n- If no context is set, the proxy raises a `RuntimeError` with `_no_app_msg` for `active_app`, `g` and `app_ctx`, or `_no_req_msg` for `request` and `session` (`globals.py:33-56`).\n\nIn this repo, the request context has been merged into `AppContext`. That's why `request` hangs off the same variable. The old `request_ctx` name is only a deprecated alias for `app_ctx` (`globals.py:65-75`).",
